            <ArticleTitle>A case study of unusual benign osseous lesion of the L5 lamina.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>107-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3233/BMR-130413</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The differential diagnosis of benign-appearing posterior element lesions is complex.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore the etiology of an unusual benign lesion found on the lamina of L5 described in a case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report. The patient's medical, surgical and imaging histories as well as medical literature were reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A 35-year old male complained of low back pain radiating to both legs for 5 months. Abdominal CT scan 1 year prior as well as post symptom-onset CT and MRI showed a well-corticated lesion lateral to the spinous process of L5. After failed rehabilitation and injection, operation revealed normal cortical bone. Pain was not markedly relieved post-operatively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The pre-surgical differential diagnosis of this lesion included osteosarcoma and osteoid osteoma, however benign pathological results suggest the possibility of atypical spina bifida occulta, Baastrup changes, atypical sacralization of L5, or a resolved fracture with heterotopic ossification.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
